WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.797
Ordinary cryptographic hashes produce fixed-length digests from input data.

2
00:00:04.797 --> 00:00:12.920
They are useful for integrity checks, identifiers, change detection, and other workflows when the design fits their properties.

3
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:20.659
A hash can help detect that data changed, but it does not hide the original data in the way encryption is intended to do.

4
00:00:20.659 --> 00:00:23.856
Hashes are not encryption and cannot be decrypted.

5
00:00:23.856 --> 00:00:26.607
That distinction matters in design reviews.

6
00:00:26.607 --> 00:00:31.851
If a team needs to recover the original value, ordinary hashing is the wrong tool.

7
00:00:31.851 --> 00:00:41.637
If a team needs to compare whether the same input was seen again, a hash may be useful, but the privacy and reversibility implications still need review.

8
00:00:41.637 --> 00:00:49.376
A hash alone does not prove authenticity unless it is part of a trusted protocol or combined with a protected secret key.

9
00:00:49.376 --> 00:00:56.156
HMAC provides integrity and authenticity when both parties share the secret key and that key is protected.

10
00:00:56.156 --> 00:01:02.999
The receiver can verify that the data matches the MAC and that the MAC was produced with the shared secret.

11
00:01:02.999 --> 00:01:06.964
Password hashing is a different problem from ordinary hashing.

12
00:01:06.964 --> 00:01:11.250
Password storage must account for low-entropy human-chosen secrets.

13
00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:17.965
Good password hashing uses salts and deliberate work factors to make large-scale guessing more expensive.

14
00:01:17.965 --> 00:01:23.082
Fast hashes such as SHA-256 are not suitable for password storage by themselves.

15
00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:24.361
New ASP.

16
00:01:24.361 --> 00:01:32.484
NET Core password-based applications should use framework-supported password hashing rather than custom password storage logic.

17
00:01:32.484 --> 00:01:42.014
Framework behavior is designed for the password storage problem, includes format and upgrade considerations, and benefits from maintenance over time.

18
00:01:42.014 --> 00:01:46.427
The practical review question is what kind of value is being handled.

19
00:01:46.427 --> 00:01:51.799
Is this a file integrity digest, a keyed authenticity check, or a password verifier.

20
00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:56.916
Each case has different storage, keying, comparison, and lifecycle expectations.
